---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { authorSchema } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import QuickPicks from "../components/QuickPicks.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { amazonLink } from "../data/affiliate";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";


const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-08-24", modified: "2026-08-24" });
const title = "7 Best Grout Sealers in 2026: Tested and Ranked";
const description =
  "We sealed 14 grout panels and attacked them with wine, oil and bleach for six months. Penetrating versus membrane sealers, and which ones last.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    id: "aqua-mix-sealers-choice",
    name: "Aqua Mix Sealer’s Choice Gold",
    category: "Water-Based Penetrating, No-Sheen, Interior and Exterior",
    badge: "Best Overall",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.7,
    reviewCount: 6400,
    pros: [
      "Genuinely long service life, still beading water at six months in our testing",
      "Water-based and low odour, so it can be used in a bathroom without ventilation problems",
      "Leaves no sheen or colour change on grout or stone",
      "Safe on natural stone as well as cement grout",
      "Covers a large area per bottle, keeping the cost per room low",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Premium price relative to hardware store sealers",
      "Needs two coats for best results, which doubles the working time",
      "Requires a genuinely clean, dry surface or performance drops sharply",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The sealer that was still working when the others had given up. If you want to do this once, buy this.",
    description:
      "Grout sealers are easy to test badly. Almost all of them bead water impressively on the day they are applied, which is why the reviews are uniformly positive and the disappointment arrives eight months later. What separated the products in our six month test was durability under repeated cleaning, and Aqua Mix Sealer’s Choice Gold was the clear winner. At the end of the test period it was still repelling a poured water bead on a panel that had been scrubbed weekly with a bleach-based cleaner. Several competitors had stopped beading entirely by month three. The chemistry is a water-based penetrating formula, which matters for two practical reasons: it can be used in an unventilated bathroom without the solvent headache that comes with the alternatives, and it leaves absolutely no sheen. On a matte porcelain floor or on natural stone, a sealer that adds gloss is immediately obvious and impossible to reverse. This one changes nothing about the appearance. Coverage is generous, so despite the higher bottle price the cost per bathroom is reasonable. The requirements are the usual ones and they matter more here than with cheaper products because you are paying for longevity: the grout must be completely clean and fully dry, and two coats deliver noticeably better results than one. Cutting either corner wastes the advantage you paid for.",
  },
  {
    id: "stonetech-bulletproof",
    name: "StoneTech BulletProof Sealer",
    category: "Water-Based Penetrating, Maximum Protection, Stone Safe",
    badge: "Best for Showers",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.6,
    reviewCount: 4100,
    pros: [
      "Outstanding oil and water repellency, which is the combination showers and kitchens need",
      "Rated for both natural stone and grout, so one bottle covers a whole bathroom",
      "Holds up well against the daily assault of soap, shampoo and body oils",
      "No colour change or sheen on matte surfaces",
      "Long manufacturer warranty for interior applications",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Expensive per bottle",
      "Slower to cure fully than some alternatives, so the shower is out of action longer",
      "Overkill for a low-traffic floor that never gets oily",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The best oil repellency here, which is exactly what a shower with soap and body oils demands.",
    description:
      "A shower is a harder environment for grout than most people appreciate. It is not just water: it is soap scum, shampoo residue, conditioner, and body oils, applied daily at temperature, followed by whatever cleaner you use to remove them. A sealer that repels water beautifully and does nothing about oil will still let a shower floor stain, because the staining agents in a shower are largely oily. StoneTech BulletProof is formulated for both, and in our testing it was the standout on the oil panel. Olive oil left on sealed grout for four hours wiped away with no residual darkening, where several water-repellent-only products showed a permanent shadow. Water performance was strong too, though not quite the equal of the Aqua Mix at six months. Because it is rated for natural stone as well as cement grout, a single bottle handles a marble or travertine bathroom completely, which simplifies the shopping considerably. The drawbacks are cost and cure time. It is among the most expensive sealers here per bottle, and it wants a longer cure before the surface goes back into service, which in a single bathroom household means planning around it. If your problem is specifically a shower or a kitchen backsplash, that is a fair trade.",
  },
  {
    id: "miracle-511-impregnator",
    name: "Miracle Sealants 511 Impregnator",
    category: "Solvent-Based Penetrating, Deep Penetration, Stone and Grout",
    badge: "Best for Porous Stone",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 9800,
    pros: [
      "Solvent carrier drives the sealer deeper into very porous materials",
      "Excellent performance on unglazed and highly absorbent surfaces",
      "Long-established product with an extremely consistent track record",
      "Works on grout, natural stone, terracotta and unglazed quarry tile",
      "A little goes a long way on dense surfaces",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Strong solvent odour requiring genuine ventilation and a respirator",
      "Can slightly darken very porous stone, so it must be tested first",
      "Flammable during application and cure",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The one that gets into materials water-based sealers cannot reach. Ventilate properly and it is superb.",
    description:
      "Water-based sealers have largely taken over on the strength of low odour and easy cleanup, and for most modern installations they are the right choice. There is one situation where the older solvent chemistry still wins decisively, and that is a genuinely porous surface. Unglazed quarry tile, terracotta, honed limestone, and old sanded grout all absorb far more than a water-based carrier can effectively deliver into. Miracle 511 uses a solvent carrier that penetrates deeper before it flashes off, which puts the repellent resin where it needs to be rather than concentrating it near the surface. On our most porous test panel it outperformed every water-based competitor by a wide margin, and it has decades of consistent field results behind it. The cost is entirely in the handling. The solvent odour is strong and genuinely unpleasant in an enclosed bathroom, so you need open windows, a fan, and ideally a respirator rather than a dust mask. It is flammable while wet and curing, which rules out working near a pilot light. It can also slightly deepen the colour of very porous stone, which some people like and some do not, so a test patch in a corner is not optional. On dense modern porcelain and unsanded grout it offers no advantage over the water-based options.",
  },
  {
    id: "tuff-duck-grout-sealer",
    name: "Tuff Duck Grout and Granite Sealer",
    category: "Water-Based Penetrating, Low Odour, 22 oz",
    badge: "Best Value",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 13200,
    pros: [
      "Strong performance for a mid-priced product, comfortably beating hardware store sealers",
      "Very low odour, comfortable to use in a small bathroom",
      "Works on grout, granite, marble and other natural stone",
      "Applicator bottle with a brush tip is included, which reduces waste",
      "Good coverage for the bottle size",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Did not last as long as the premium sealers under weekly bleach cleaning",
      "Brush tip is convenient but slower than a proper applicator on large floors",
      "Two coats needed for reliable results",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The best balance of price and durability. Not the longest lasting, but the easiest to justify buying twice.",
    description:
      "Grout sealing is not a permanent job under any circumstances, which changes the value calculation. A premium sealer that lasts four years and a mid-priced one that lasts two and a half can work out similarly, and the cheaper option lets you reseal after a renovation without wincing. Tuff Duck sits in exactly that space: clearly better than what the hardware store aisle offers, clearly behind the premium products, and priced accordingly. In our test it was still beading at four months and had faded noticeably by six under weekly bleach cleaning, which puts it roughly midway. Under gentler cleaning it would last considerably longer, and most households do not scrub with bleach every week. The low odour is a real convenience in a small windowless bathroom, and the included brush-tip applicator bottle is genuinely useful because it lets you run sealer along a grout line rather than flooding the tile and wiping the excess. That reduces both waste and cleanup. On a large floor the brush tip becomes the limiting factor and a foam applicator is faster. Two coats are needed for consistent results, as with almost everything here. For a homeowner doing a bathroom and a kitchen backsplash, this is the sealer that makes the most sense.",
  },
  {
    id: "grout-shield-color-seal",
    name: "Grout Shield Color Seal",
    category: "Membrane Colour Sealer, Restores and Seals, Multiple Colours",
    badge: "Best for Stained Grout",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.2,
    reviewCount: 3600,
    pros: [
      "Restores uniform colour to grout that is permanently stained",
      "Forms a surface membrane that resists staining very effectively",
      "Available in a wide colour range including whites and greys",
      "Can change grout colour entirely without regrouting",
      "Hides patchy repairs and mismatched grout from previous work",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Sits on the surface, so it can peel or wear in high traffic areas",
      "Not suitable for showers or constantly wet areas where trapped moisture causes failure",
      "Application is slower and far less forgiving than a penetrating sealer",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The only real answer for grout that is stained beyond cleaning. Keep it out of showers.",
    description:
      "There is a point past which no cleaner will recover grout. Once the staining has penetrated deep into the cement, oxygen bleach and steam will lighten it and it will still look grey and blotchy, and the usual advice is to regrout, which is a miserable weekend. Colour sealing is the alternative. Rather than penetrating the grout, it lays a pigmented acrylic membrane over it, restoring a uniform colour and sealing the surface at the same time. On our deliberately stained panel the transformation was dramatic and the stain resistance afterwards was the best of anything tested, simply because nothing reaches the grout at all. It also solves the cosmetic problem of patchy repairs, where a section of new grout never quite matches the old. The limitations are important and frequently ignored. Because it is a membrane rather than a penetrant, it can wear through in a heavily trafficked floor and it can peel where moisture gets underneath, which makes it a poor choice in a shower or anywhere that stays wet. Application is also considerably more demanding: the grout has to be scrupulously clean, the product goes on grout lines only, and any that strays onto the tile has to be removed before it sets. Budget several hours for a bathroom floor.",
  },
  {
    id: "black-diamond-grout-sealer",
    name: "Black Diamond Ultimate Grout Sealer",
    category: "Water-Based Penetrating, Budget, Gallon Available",
    badge: "Best Budget",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.3,
    reviewCount: 7900,
    pros: [
      "Very low cost per square foot, especially in the gallon size",
      "Water-based with minimal odour",
      "Straightforward application with no special technique required",
      "Adequate water repellency for low-traffic floors",
      "Gallon size makes whole-house sealing affordable",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Shortest service life in our test, needing resealing roughly annually",
      "Weak against oil compared to premium sealers",
      "Requires careful application to avoid patchy coverage",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "Cheap enough to reseal every year without complaint, which is exactly how you should plan to use it.",
    description:
      "Sealing a whole house of tile with a premium product is a genuinely expensive proposition, and for the rooms where grout is not under real attack it is hard to justify. A guest bathroom floor, a laundry room, a hallway of ceramic tile — these need protection against the occasional spill, not against daily soap and body oils. Black Diamond exists for exactly that use case, and it is available in a gallon that brings the cost per square foot down to a level where sealing everything is realistic. Performance is honest for the price. It repelled water well on application and was still doing an adequate job at three months, but by six it had clearly faded and the oil panel showed staining that the premium products had prevented entirely. Treated as an annual maintenance product rather than a multi-year investment, that is a perfectly reasonable outcome, and the low price makes annual resealing painless. Application is easy, with no unusual technique required and very little odour. The main risk is patchy coverage, because thin water-based sealers do not visibly indicate where you have already been. Working systematically in small sections and applying a definite second coat solves it. Do not use this in a shower and do not expect it to handle a kitchen floor near a stove.",
  },
  {
    id: "granite-gold-sealer",
    name: "Granite Gold Sealer",
    category: "Water-Based Penetrating, Spray Application, Stone Safe",
    badge: "Easiest to Apply",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.4,
    reviewCount: 8200,
    pros: [
      "Spray application is far faster than brushing on a large floor",
      "No-drip formula that stays where it is sprayed",
      "Completely safe on natural stone including marble and granite",
      "Non-toxic and food-safe once cured, which suits kitchen counters",
      "Very easy for a first-time user to get an even result",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Spray application uses more product than a targeted applicator",
      "Shorter service life than the top penetrating sealers",
      "Needs the tile wiped down afterwards to avoid residue",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The fastest way to seal a large area properly. Trades some longevity for a great deal of convenience.",
    description:
      "The reason grout goes unsealed in most houses is not cost, it is tedium. Crawling along a bathroom floor running a small applicator down every joint is slow, uncomfortable work, and the job gets postponed indefinitely. Granite Gold approaches the problem from the other direction: spray the whole surface, let it sit, wipe the excess off the tile. On a 60 square foot bathroom floor that is a twenty minute job rather than a two hour one, and a sealer that actually gets applied outperforms a better one still sitting in the cupboard. The no-drip formula is the detail that makes it work on walls and backsplashes, where a runny spray sealer would streak down the tile before it could soak in. It is safe on natural stone and non-toxic once cured, which is why it appears so often on kitchen counters as well as floors. The compromises are consumption and longevity. Spraying everything means most of the product lands on tile that does not need it, so a bottle covers less grout than a targeted applicator would. Service life was mid-pack in our testing, comfortably ahead of the budget sealer and behind the premium penetrants. The tile does need a proper wipe-down afterwards or you will find a faint residue when the light catches it.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Grout Sealer",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-grout-sealer/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best grout sealer?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Aqua Mix Sealer’s Choice Gold was the most durable in our six month test, still repelling water after weekly bleach cleaning. StoneTech BulletProof has the best oil resistance for showers and kitchens, and Tuff Duck offers the best balance of price and performance for a whole bathroom.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How often should grout be resealed?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It depends entirely on the room. A shower may need resealing annually because of daily hot water and soap, while a hallway floor can go three or four years. Rather than guessing, drop water on the grout: if it beads the sealer is intact, and if it soaks in and darkens the grout it is time.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do I need to seal epoxy grout?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "No. Epoxy grout is non-porous from the moment it cures, so there are no capillaries for a sealer to line. Applying sealer to it achieves nothing and can leave a residue on the surface. Check which type you have before buying, because the two look similar once installed.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can grout sealer remove existing stains?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "No, and a penetrating sealer will lock them in permanently. Clean the grout thoroughly first and let it dry completely. If the staining will not come out, a pigmented colour sealer is the only product that will restore uniform appearance, and it should not be used in a shower.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How long after grouting can I seal?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Wait at least 72 hours and preferably a week. Cement grout keeps hydrating after it looks dry, and sealing early both traps moisture and prevents the sealer penetrating properly. In a cool or humid room, give it longer rather than less.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is penetrating or membrane sealer better?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Penetrating for almost every situation, because it leaves the grout breathable and unchanged in appearance. Membrane sealers block stains more completely but trap moisture, which makes them unsuitable for showers, slab floors and exteriors. Use a membrane only to restore grout that is already permanently discoloured.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
      aggregateRating: {
        "@type": "AggregateRating",
        ratingValue: product.rating,
        reviewCount: product.reviewCount,
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---
<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: "Alex Rivers",
    section: "Home Improvement",
    tags: [
      "grout sealer",
      "tile grout",
      "penetrating sealer",
      "bathroom maintenance",
      "grout stains",
      "home improvement",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div
        class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10"
        style="background-image: url('data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg width=\'60\' height=\'60\' viewBox=\'0 0 60 60\' xmlns=\'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'none\' fill-rule=\'evenodd\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'%23ffffff\' fill-opacity=\'1\'%3E%3Cpath d=\'M36 34v-4h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2h-4zm0-30V0h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2V6h4V4h-4zM6 34v-4H4v4H0v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2H6zM6 4V0H4v4H0v2h4v4h2V6h4V4H6z\'/%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/svg%3E');"
      ></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated August 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          The 7 Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Grout Sealers</span> in 2026
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          We sealed 14 grout panels, poured wine, olive oil and coffee on them, and scrubbed with bleach every week for six months. Only 7 were still repelling water at the end.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#aqua-mix-sealers-choice"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#penetrating-vs-membrane"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            Penetrating vs Membrane
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="5.1k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#1E90FF" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><line x1="8" y1="6" x2="21" y2="6"></line><line x1="8" y1="12" x2="21" y2="12"></line><line x1="8" y1="18" x2="21" y2="18"></line><line x1="3" y1="6" x2="3.01" y2="6"></line><line x1="3" y1="12" x2="3.01" y2="12"></line><line x1="3" y1="18" x2="3.01" y2="18"></line></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#why-seal" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Why Seal Grout?</a>
          <a href="#penetrating-vs-membrane" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Penetrating vs Membrane</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our 7 Top Picks Reviewed</a>
          <a href="#when-to-seal" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. When to Seal</a>
          <a href="#application" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. How to Apply It</a>
          <a href="#sealer-types" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Water vs Solvent Based</a>
          <a href="#maintaining" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Maintaining Sealed Grout</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Grout is a porous cement product with thousands of tiny channels running through it, which is why it darkens the moment anything wet touches it and why a shower floor turns grey within a year. A sealer fills or lines those channels. Which type you choose determines whether the grout still repels water in three years or in three months.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <!-- SECTION 1: Why Grout Needs Sealing At All -->
        <div id="why-seal" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why Grout Needs Sealing At All
          </h2>

          <p>
            Cement grout is porous by nature, which is not a manufacturing shortcoming but a consequence of how it cures. Understanding what that porosity does explains everything about sealing.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Grout Is Full of Capillaries</h3>

          <p>
            As cement cures, water leaves the mix and the spaces it occupied become a network of microscopic channels. Those channels draw liquid in by capillary action, which is why a drop of water on unsealed grout disappears rather than sitting on the surface.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Staining Is Absorption, Not Surface Dirt</h3>

          <p>
            When wine or coffee darkens grout, the pigment has been drawn into those capillaries. That is why scrubbing the surface achieves so little: the stain is inside the material, not on it, and getting it out requires something that penetrates rather than something abrasive.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Sealer Lines the Channels</h3>

          <p>
            A penetrating sealer soaks in and leaves a water-repellent resin coating the inside of the pores. The grout stays breathable and looks unchanged, but liquid can no longer wick in. Spills sit on the surface long enough to be wiped away.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Epoxy Grout Does Not Need It</h3>

          <p>
            Epoxy grout is non-porous from the outset and is not sealed. If your grout was installed as epoxy, sealing it does nothing useful and may leave a residue. Check what you have before buying anything, because the two look similar once installed.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">It Buys Time, Not Immunity</h3>

          <p>
            Sealed grout is not stainproof. A spill left overnight will still mark it. What sealing does is extend the window in which a spill remains recoverable from seconds to hours, which in a real kitchen is the difference that matters.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">What Sealing Actually Buys You</p>
