        <!-- SECTION 2: Penetrating vs Membrane Sealers -->
        <div id="penetrating-vs-membrane" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Penetrating vs Membrane Sealers
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every grout sealer works in one of two ways, and choosing the wrong one for the room is the most consequential mistake in this category.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Penetrating sealers soak in and leave nothing on top.</strong> The resin lines the pores from within, so the grout looks exactly as it did before, keeps its texture, and remains breathable. This is what belongs in a shower, on a floor, and anywhere moisture might come from behind or below.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Membrane sealers form a film on the surface.</strong> They block staining extremely effectively because nothing reaches the grout at all, and pigmented versions can restore colour to grout that is permanently discoloured. The film is the strength and the weakness simultaneously.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Breathability is the deciding factor.</strong> Grout on a shower wall, a slab floor, or an exterior surface has moisture arriving from behind it. A membrane traps that moisture, which lifts the film and can cause worse problems than the staining it prevented. Penetrating sealers let it pass.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Use a membrane only where the grout is beyond saving.</strong> Colour sealing is the right answer for a kitchen floor whose grout is permanently blotchy, because the alternative is regrouting. It is the wrong answer for a shower, no matter how bad the shower looks.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Sheen is the other giveaway.</strong> Penetrating sealers change nothing about appearance. Anything that adds gloss is sitting on the surface, which tells you what kind of product you have even if the label is vague.
          </p>
        </div>

