        <!-- SECTION 4: Timing: New Grout, Old Grout and Resealing -->
        <div id="when-to-seal" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Timing: New Grout, Old Grout and Resealing
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sealing at the wrong moment wastes the product entirely, and the timing rules are simple but easy to get wrong.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>New grout needs to cure first.</strong> Cement grout continues hydrating for days after it looks dry, and sealing it early traps moisture and prevents proper penetration. Three days is the common minimum and a week is safer, particularly in a cool or humid room.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Old grout has to be genuinely clean.</strong> Sealer locks in whatever is there, including a stain you were hoping it would hide. Clean thoroughly, let it dry completely, and accept that badly stained grout needs colour sealing or regrouting rather than a penetrating sealer.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Dry means properly dry, not surface dry.</strong> Grout that looks dry can still hold moisture in its pores, and a water-based sealer applied onto it will not penetrate. Give it a full day after cleaning, longer in a bathroom with poor ventilation.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Test whether resealing is needed with water.</strong> Drop water onto the grout line. If it beads and sits, the sealer is still working. If it soaks in and darkens the grout within a minute or two, it is time to reseal. This takes ten seconds and is more reliable than any calendar.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Expect showers to need it more often.</strong> Daily hot water, soap and cleaning products degrade sealer faster than anything else in a house. Annually is realistic in a shower, where a hallway floor might genuinely go four years.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">The Ten Second Test</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              Drop a few beads of water onto a grout line and wait two minutes. If the water sits on top, your sealer is intact. If the grout darkens where the water touched, it is absorbing and needs resealing. Do this in the shower, on the kitchen floor and in the hallway — the answers will differ, and that tells you where to spend your time.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 5: Applying Sealer Properly -->
        <div id="application" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Applying Sealer Properly
          </h2>

          <p>
            Application technique separates a sealer that lasts years from the same product failing in months, and the steps that matter are the ones people skip.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Clean first, and let it dry fully.</strong> Any residue, soap film or cleaning product left on the grout blocks penetration. Rinse thoroughly after cleaning, because cleaner residue is itself a barrier, then allow a full day of drying.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Apply to the grout, not the whole floor.</strong> A brush-tip applicator or a small foam brush run along each joint puts the product where it is needed and dramatically reduces both waste and cleanup. Spray application is faster but uses far more product.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Let it dwell, then wipe the tile.</strong> The sealer needs several minutes in the grout to penetrate, but any left sitting on glazed tile will dry to a haze that is unpleasant to remove. Work in small sections so nothing dries before you get back to it.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Two coats, with the recommended wait between.</strong> The second coat fills what the first missed, and almost every manufacturer specifies it. Single-coat application is the most common reason a good sealer underperforms.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Ventilate, especially with solvent-based products.</strong> Solvent penetrating sealers need open windows and a fan, and a respirator rather than a dust mask. Water-based products are far more pleasant but still benefit from airflow to cure.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Keep the surface dry through the full cure.</strong> Most sealers want at least 24 hours before the area gets wet, and showers often need longer. Using a shower the same evening is the fastest way to waste an afternoon of work.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6: Water-Based vs Solvent-Based Chemistry -->
        <div id="sealer-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Water-Based vs Solvent-Based Chemistry
          </h2>

          <p>
            Within penetrating sealers, the carrier that delivers the resin into the grout splits the category, and each has situations where it is clearly correct.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Water-based sealers are the modern default.</strong> Low odour, easy cleanup, no flammability, and safe to use in an unventilated bathroom. Modern formulations perform very well on dense porcelain and standard cement grout, which covers the majority of installations.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Solvent-based sealers penetrate deeper.</strong> The solvent carries resin further into the material before evaporating, which matters on genuinely porous surfaces like terracotta, unglazed quarry tile, honed limestone and old sanded grout.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Solvent products demand real ventilation.</strong> The odour is strong, the vapour is flammable during application and cure, and a bathroom with the door shut is not an acceptable workspace. Open windows, a fan and a respirator are the minimum.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Colour change is a real risk with solvents.</strong> Deep-penetrating solvent sealers can slightly darken porous stone, an effect sometimes marketed as colour enhancement. Whether you want it is a matter of taste, but discovering it after doing the whole floor is not.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Test both in a corner.</strong> Whatever the label promises, apply a small patch somewhere inconspicuous and let it cure before committing to a whole room. Ten minutes of testing prevents a floor you dislike looking at.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 7: Keeping Sealed Grout Working -->
        <div id="maintaining" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Keeping Sealed Grout Working
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sealer is degraded by cleaning far more than by use, which means the products under your sink largely determine how long the job lasts.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Acidic cleaners strip sealer and attack grout.</strong> Vinegar, lemon and many bathroom descalers dissolve cement over time and shorten sealer life considerably. The habit of cleaning tile with vinegar is one of the most common causes of grout deteriorating early.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Bleach degrades sealer too.</strong> It is effective against mould and it is hard on the resin, which is why our bleach-cleaned panels lost repellency faster. Use it when you need it rather than as a routine weekly cleaner.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Neutral pH cleaners are the right routine choice.</strong> They handle ordinary soil without attacking either the sealer or the grout, and they cost no more than the aggressive alternatives. This single change adds a year or more to a sealer.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Squeegee the shower.</strong> Thirty seconds after each shower removes the water that carries soap and minerals into the grout, and it reduces both staining and the frequency of resealing more than any product does.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Ventilate to control mould rather than bleaching it repeatedly.</strong> Black spotting in shower grout is mould growing in residual moisture. An extractor fan running for twenty minutes after a shower prevents it far more effectively than bleaching it away every fortnight.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Deal with grout haze before you seal.</strong> A faint cement film left behind after grouting looks like nothing until sealer goes over it, at which point it becomes a permanent cloudy patch across the tile. A haze remover used before sealing takes twenty minutes and prevents a problem that cannot be undone afterwards.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Reseal on evidence, not schedule.</strong> The water bead test takes seconds and tells you exactly where you stand. Resealing early wastes product, and resealing late means the grout has already absorbed stains that the new sealer will lock in.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 8: Frequently Asked Questions -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best grout sealer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Aqua Mix Sealer’s Choice Gold was the most durable in our six month test, still repelling water after weekly bleach cleaning. StoneTech BulletProof has the best oil resistance for showers and kitchens, and Tuff Duck offers the best balance of price and performance for a whole bathroom.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should grout be resealed?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends entirely on the room. A shower may need resealing annually because of daily hot water and soap, while a hallway floor can go three or four years. Rather than guessing, drop water on the grout: if it beads the sealer is intact, and if it soaks in and darkens the grout it is time.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need to seal epoxy grout?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Epoxy grout is non-porous from the moment it cures, so there are no capillaries for a sealer to line. Applying sealer to it achieves nothing and can leave a residue on the surface. Check which type you have before buying, because the two look similar once installed.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can grout sealer remove existing stains?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No, and a penetrating sealer will lock them in permanently. Clean the grout thoroughly first and let it dry completely. If the staining will not come out, a pigmented colour sealer is the only product that will restore uniform appearance, and it should not be used in a shower.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long after grouting can I seal?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Wait at least 72 hours and preferably a week. Cement grout keeps hydrating after it looks dry, and sealing early both traps moisture and prevents the sealer penetrating properly. In a cool or humid room, give it longer rather than less.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is penetrating or membrane sealer better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Penetrating for almost every situation, because it leaves the grout breathable and unchanged in appearance. Membrane sealers block stains more completely but trap moisture, which makes them unsuitable for showers, slab floors and exteriors. Use a membrane only to restore grout that is already permanently discoloured.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Grout Sealing Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Grout sealing fails for a small number of reasons, and almost all of them happen before the bottle is opened.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing Grout That Is Not Clean</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A penetrating sealer locks in whatever is present, so any remaining stain, soap film or cleaner residue becomes permanent and also blocks penetration. Clean thoroughly, rinse to remove the cleaner itself, and allow a full day of drying. Grout that will not come clean needs colour sealing, not a penetrating product.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a Membrane Sealer in a Shower</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Colour sealers and other film-forming products trap moisture arriving from behind the tile, which lifts the film and can cause worse damage than the staining they were meant to prevent. Showers, slab floors and exterior tile all need a penetrating sealer that leaves the grout breathable.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Applying Only One Coat</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Almost every manufacturer specifies two coats, and the second fills what the first missed. Single-coat application is the most common reason a well-reviewed sealer underperforms, and it is invisible at the time because the first coat beads water perfectly well on day one.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Cleaning Sealed Grout With Vinegar</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Acidic cleaners dissolve cement grout and strip sealer, and vinegar is the most commonly recommended one in household cleaning advice. Neutral pH cleaners handle ordinary soil without attacking either, and switching to one adds a year or more to how long a sealing job lasts.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

